From 22ce3a3072e810a41c70f27285ad65669ae6dc96 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 20:30:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] rpc : per-connection backends, tracked connections, client
 cap, keepalive

Builds on the thread-per-connection accept loop (ggml-org/llama.cpp#28916)
to make a multi-client rpc-server usable in production.

Root cause being fixed: rpc-server served one TCP connection at a time and
listened with a backlog of 1. llama-server keeps its RPC connection open
for the lifetime of the model, so once one master connected, every other
connection (a second model, `--list-devices`, k8s tcpSocket probes) hung
in the kernel backlog -- ~127 s of SYN retries then "Failed to connect",
or a HELLO that never gets a reply. Reproduced on loopback with a raw
protocol client (hold A open after HELLO, B's HELLO times out) and live on
a gabesrv10 -> gabesrv06 split (issue #28908 reports the same on 2x Strix
Halo).

#28916 serves each connection on a detached thread and serializes all
graph computes behind one global mutex. That removes the starvation but:
every client and every device still computes one at a time, detached
threads can't be counted or joined, a client that vanishes without a FIN
leaves its thread blocked in recv() forever holding all of its device
buffers, and the backlog stays at 1.

Changes:
- Each connection lazily creates its own backend instance per device on
  its first graph compute and frees it on disconnect, the same way several
  llama_contexts share one device. Buffer ops were already safe across
  threads (buffer types are per device; the Vulkan sync read/write/copy
  paths take device->mutex), so only the backend compute context needed
  isolating. Metadata commands (alignment, max size, alloc size, device
  memory, alloc) now use the device directly and never create a backend.
- `--serialize-compute` keeps the old model instead: one shared backend
  per device, computes serialized per device (not globally).
- Connections are tracked in a registry owned by the accept loop; finished
  threads are reaped and joined, and accept/close are logged with an id,
  the peer address and the active count.
- `--max-clients N` (default 8, 0 = unlimited) caps concurrent
  connections; the protocol is unauthenticated, so this bounds per-client
  threads and backend contexts.
- `--keepalive N` (default 30 s) enables TCP keepalive on accepted
  sockets, so a vanished client is dropped after ~2x N and its buffers are
  freed by ~rpc_server. No idle read timeout: a loaded model's connection
  can legitimately sit idle for hours.
- listen() backlog raised from 1 to SOMAXCONN.
- New C API ggml_backend_rpc_start_server_ex() + default params; the old
  entry point is kept and forwards to it. No wire protocol change.

Verified: loopback repro now answers B's HELLO in <1 ms while A is held,
A keeps working afterwards; test-rpc-multi-server passes.

